  "text": "**Solution 1 is more likely correct: f(n) = (n+1)/3.** I could not verify it by a full derivation, so this rests on recalled literature rather than a complete proof.\n\n**Solution 2 is unsupported.** Its construction is valid. The balanced blow-up of C₅ with head-coloring has no rainbow C₄ and minimum color degree ⌊n/5⌋+1, so it shows f(n) must exceed about n/5. But that only gives a lower bound. The claimed upper bound (n+6)/5 rests on a \"structural analysis\" that is only sketched, with no proof. It also does not rule out better constructions, and the (n+1)/3 result that Solution 1 cites would require a construction with minimum color degree near n/3 and no rainbow C₄. That is a much larger value than the C₅ blow-up reaches, so Solution 2's \"best possible\" claim is not established.\n\n**Solution 1** matches my recollection of the literature on color degree conditions for rainbow C₄ in triangle-free edge-colored graphs, where the threshold for large n is (n+1)/3 and is sharp. I have not checked a sharpness construction, so my confidence is moderate.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
